INT. RADIO TELESCOPE CONTROL ROOM - NIGHT

A concrete room at the end of a dirt road, humming with machines. Banks of dials. A wall of monitors showing a single flat grey line.

DR. SOLVEIG ABERNATHY-OKAFOR, 40s, in a sweater and fingerless gloves, sits at the main console with a voice recorder and a cold cup of tea. She hasn't slept.

She presses RECORD.

SOLVEIG
Log entry, hour sixty. Signal is still there.

She plays back the clip. A pulse, faint, like a heartbeat, from a speaker.

She rewinds.

SOLVEIG (V.O.)
(recording)
Log entry, hour fifty-two. It repeats every eleven minutes.

SOLVEIG
Eleven minutes.

She leans closer, listening, and her hand slips to the console edge. The pulse thumps. The monitors flicker. The lights dim for a heartbeat, then recover.

She exhales and lifts the recorder again.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
Hour sixty-one. The pulse has changed. It now repeats every ten.

She pauses. Her voice drops.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
Ten minutes.

She plays it back.

SOLVEIG (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Hour sixty-one. The pulse has changed.

SOLVEIG
Stop.

She stops it. Silence. Then, faintly, from the speaker, the pulse resumes. A beat. Another.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
That's not mine.

She turns slowly. The speaker is off. The monitors show the grey line. The pulse continues.

It's coming from the recorder.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
(a whisper)
Hello?

She holds the recorder up. The pulse quickens.

SOLVEIG (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording, but wrong, slow, deeper)
Hello.

She drops the recorder. It clatters. She stands, backs away. The lights flicker. The console hums. The recorder lies on its side, spinning its reels.

SOLVEIG (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording, patient)
Solveig. Come here.

She does not move. Tears gather at her eyes.

SOLVEIG
No.

SOLVEIG (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Come here. I have been waiting eleven minutes.

SOLVEIG
Ten.

SOLVEIG (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Ten.

A long silence. The pulse slows. The lights steady. The grey line on the monitors becomes a single, perfect, ordinary wave.

She picks up the recorder. Her hand is still. She looks at it.

SOLVEIG
It's gone.

She stops the machine. The room is silent. She sets the recorder down on the console, very gently, like a bird she has learned to trust.

She picks up her tea. It's warm.
